# Decided
- Placement (trueqbit: "all singular-named header files should rather
  not create file-level comments, if possible place the purpose on the
  main carrier"; "ast/ + schema/ stands" - a node header holds an
  internal node struct with its public factory, often a family). Claude
  worded the test as the carrier, the name as its sign; recorded in
  campaign.md "Form". On the carrier: statement_serializer,
  serializer_context, order_by_serializer, table_name_collector,
  column_expressions_collector (Claude's lean on trueqbit's open
  question: the struct does the expanding, collect_column_expressions is
  its entry), serialize_default_value, ast_iterator, node_tuple. @file:
  quoting.h, streaming.h, cte_column_names.h (renamed for this, 941a3e30).
- Purposes, Claude's leans taken ("otherwise your leans"), with two
  changes by trueqbit:
  - serializer_context: "hold the schema and the settings a
    serialization runs under." judged "a tiny bit too detailed" - it
    sums up members; now "carry the context of a serialization." The
    guard eval is deferred, recorded in handover-feedback-domain-sources.md
    (sixth).
  - ast_iterator: Claude offered "visit"; trueqbit: a specific form of
    visitation, a traversal of a tree's elements with rules, "iteration"
    used interchangeably, and "traversal" fits documentation better.
    Claude agreed: "visitation" reads in C++ as the Visitor pattern or
    std::visit, double dispatch, which this is not; "iterate" in the tree
    means "call for each element" (iterate_tuple), so the name stays.
    Now "traverse the nodes of an expression tree."
- Checked against sqlite.org (decision 4 a): ORDER BY, ordering terms -
  https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html#orderby; CTE column names -
  https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html (cte-table-name, its optional
  column-name list); identifiers and literal values -
  https://www.sqlite.org/lang_keywords.html,
  https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html#literal_values_constants_.
- Every Intention sentence checked against the includes and the files
  it names: serializer_context is created in execution/storage.h and
  result_set/mapped_view.h, result_set_view.h, kept by
  dynamic_order_by_t (ast/order_by.h:150 serializes on push) and
  dynamic_set_t; the storage compares dflt_value at
  execution/storage_base.h:1508; streaming.h escapes on its own and
  includes no quoting.h, so quoting.h names the serializer, its helpers
  (column_expressions_collector.h) and the storage (storage_base.h);
  get_prepared_statement.h computes a bound value's index through
  node_tuple and finds it through iterate_ast; add-dsl-node's SKILL.md
  lists statement_serializer, ast_iterator and node_tuple.
- Text cut or moved:
  - quoting.h, its @file line, now the Purpose; the member list cut:
    "/** @file Quoting and escaping of SQL text: identifiers, string and
    blob literals. */"
  - column_expressions_collector: "/** @short Column expression
    collector. */" - repeats the name.
  - serialize_default_value: "Serialize a column's default value." -
    the Purpose says it.
  - ast_iterator, the old block:
      ast_iterator accepts any expression and a callable object
      which will be called for any node of provided expression.
      E.g. if we pass `where(is_equal(5, max(&User::id, 10))` then
      callable object will be called with 5, &User::id and 10.
      ast_iterator is used in finding literals to be bound to
      a statement, and to collect table names.
      Note that not all leaves of the expression tree are visited:
      Column expressions can be more complex, but are passed as a whole
      to the callable. Examples are `column_pointer<>` and
      `alias_qualified_column_t<>`.
      To use `ast_iterator` call `iterate_ast(object, callable);`
      `T` is an ast element, e.g. where_t
    Placed: the first sentence in the Purpose; the uses in the
    Intention; the example, the column expressions and iterate_ast
    reworded into a paragraph below it, positive (rule wording: "reach
    the callable whole, rather than leaf by leaf"), the example's
    missing parenthesis closed; "T is an ast element" cut - node_type
    says it.
